<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Sakai</h1><p class="st-meta">1470 CE – 1569 CE (approx.) · capital Sakai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A self-governing merchant port near Osaka that Jesuit visitors compared to Venice, a centre of overseas trade, gunmaking and the tea ceremony.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sakai grew as a port on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-bay">Osaka Bay</a> and became a terminus of the official trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming China</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Onin War</a> made the routes through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Hyogo</a> unsafe from about 1469. Wealthy merchant houses took over the town's administration as central authority collapsed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of leading merchants, the egoshu, governed the town, which was protected by moats and hired its own guards. Sakai traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom</a> and later the Portuguese, and became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan's</a> main producers of firearms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> masters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeno-joo">Takeno Joo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu">Sen no Rikyu</a> came from its merchant class, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspar-vilela">Gaspar Vilela</a> described it as a republic like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1568 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> demanded a huge war levy; Sakai at first resisted but submitted in 1569 and came under his direct control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> later filled in its moats and moved many merchants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sakai.jpg" alt="Sakai" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Sakai Yayoi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Sakai_Lighthouse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1470 CE – 1569 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sakai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Free city of Sakai, Sakai city-state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1520&amp;focus=sakai" aria-label="Sakai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8122 926 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sakai lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8442 1182L8448 1163"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8448 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8442 1182h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8442 1182h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1520&amp;focus=sakai">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

